        Consider water flowing steadily through a horizontal pipe of length <InlineEquation equation="L" /> and
        diameter <InlineEquation equation="D" />. The pipe contains one entrance, two elbows, one valve, and one
        exit. The average velocity in the pipe is <InlineEquation equation="V" />.
        The goal is to estimate the total pressure drop caused by both major and minor
        losses.
    </SubSubSection>
        <SubSubSubSection title="Symbolic Setup">
            The major loss due to wall friction is:

            <DisplayEquation equation="h_f = f\\frac{L}{D}\\frac{V^2}{2g}" title="" />

            The minor losses are written using loss coefficients:

            <DisplayEquation equation="h_m = \\sum K\\frac{V^2}{2g}" title="" />

            For this pipe system:

            <DisplayEquation equation="\\sum K = K_{\\text{entrance}} + 2K_{\\text{elbow}} + K_{\\text{valve}} + K_{\\text{exit}}" title="" />

            Thus, the total head loss is:

            <DisplayEquation equation="h_{L,\\text{total}} = \\left( f\\frac{L}{D} + K_{\\text{entrance}} + 2K_{\\text{elbow}} + K_{\\text{valve}} + K_{\\text{exit}} \\right) \\frac{V^2}{2g}" title="" />

            The corresponding pressure drop is:

            <DisplayEquation equation="\\Delta p = \\rho g h_{L,\\text{total}}" title="" />

            or:

            <DisplayEquation equation="\\Delta p = \\left( f\\frac{L}{D} + \\sum K \\right) \\frac{\\rho V^2}{2}" title="" />
        </SubSubSubSection>
        <SubSubSubSection title="Numerical Example">
            Suppose:

            <DisplayEquation equation="L=20\\ \\text{m}, \\qquad D=0.05\\ \\text{m}, \\qquad V=2.0\\ \\text{m/s}" title="" />


            <DisplayEquation equation="f=0.024, \\qquad \\rho=1000\\ \\text{kg/m}^3" title="" />

            and the loss coefficients are:

            <DisplayEquation equation="K_{\\text{entrance}}=0.5, \\qquad K_{\\text{elbow}}=0.3, \\qquad K_{\\text{valve}}=2.0, \\qquad K_{\\text{exit}}=1.0" title="" />

            First compute the total minor-loss coefficient:

            <DisplayEquation equation="\\sum K = 0.5+2(0.3)+2.0+1.0 = 4.1" title="" />

            The major-loss coefficient is:

            <DisplayEquation equation="f\\frac{L}{D} = 0.024\\left(\\frac{20}{0.05}\\right) = 9.6" title="" />

            Therefore:

            <DisplayEquation equation="f\\frac{L}{D}+\\sum K = 9.6+4.1 = 13.7" title="" />

            The dynamic pressure term is:

            <DisplayEquation equation="\\frac{\\rho V^2}{2} = \\frac{1000(2.0)^2}{2} = 2000\\ \\text{Pa}" title="" />

            Thus, the total pressure drop is:

            <DisplayEquation equation="\\Delta p = 13.7(2000) = 27400\\ \\text{Pa}" title="" />

            or:

            <DisplayEquation equation="\\Delta p = 27.4\\ \\text{kPa}" title="" />

            This example shows that the total pressure drop comes from both pipe-wall
            friction and localized losses from components. Even though they are called
            minor losses, the fittings and valve contribute a significant portion of the
            overall loss.

        </SubSubSubSection>
